# Force LF on everything that gets executed or parsed as code.
#
# Why: a CRLF in a shebang (`#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`) makes the kernel look for
# an interpreter literally named "bash\r" and fail with a cryptic
# "no such file or directory". Python sources, shell scripts, the agw shim, and
# the YAML/JSON policy files must stay LF regardless of the host's
# core.autocrlf setting (Windows checkouts otherwise corrupt them).
* text=auto eol=lf

*.py    text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
# A gitattributes pattern containing a slash is anchored to this file's
# directory, so the old `bin/*` matched a repo-root `bin/` that has never
# existed. The shim actually lives here.
plugin/bin/* text eol=lf

# Batch files are the one exception, and must come after `plugin/bin/*` to win.
#
# Why: cmd.exe does not read a batch file into memory. It seeks by byte offset
# and re-reads after every command, so `goto :label` and parenthesised blocks
# resolve against file positions. An LF-only batch file shifts those offsets
# and is a known source of intermittent "label not found" and block-parsing
# failures. agw.cmd uses both constructs.
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf

# Safety-lab fixtures are byte-exact test inputs: the product-plan.gdoc stub
# mimics a Windows-authored Google Docs stub, and the snapshot/restore probes
# assert on exact byte counts. Never normalize them. (This line named
# `synthetic/cowork-safety-lab/`, a path that has never existed, so the
# fixtures were unprotected on Windows checkouts.)
synthetic/safety-lab/workspace/** -text
